{
  "module": "Attention: how tokens exchange information",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "scaling-and-cost",
    "title": "Scaling the scores, and the n² cost",
    "summary": "Why the scores are divided by the square root of d_k before softmax, and why attention costs grow with the square of the sequence length.",
    "covers": ["score-scaling", "attention-cost"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "scores-weights",
        "reason": "Both are properties of the score matrix and of the softmax that follows it."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "big-scores",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A dot product adds up d_k products. The more terms there are, the larger the typical result."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "If the entries of a query and a key are roughly independent, with zero mean and unit variance, the dot product has "
            },
            {
              "text": "variance d_k",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". Its typical size grows like "
            },
            {
              "text": "√d_k",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 32)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "saturation",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "With d_k = 64, a typical score is around 8 in size. Suppose one query gives the scores 8, 0, 0. Softmax gives 0.9993, 0.0003, 0.0003."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Nearly all the weight lands on one position. This is "
            },
            {
              "text": "saturation",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": the softmax is almost a hard choice, and its gradients nearly vanish, which makes learning hard."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 32)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "divide",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The fix is to divide the scores by √d_k before softmax. This brings their variance back to 1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "\\mathrm{softmax}\\!\\left(\\frac{Q K^{\\top}}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)",
          "spoken": "softmax of Q times K transposed, divided by the square root of d k"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "In the example, dividing by √64 = 8 turns the scores into 1, 0, 0, and softmax gives 0.576, 0.212, 0.212. The attention is still focused, but no longer all on one position."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 32, 31)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "n-squared",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Now the cost. The score matrix has one entry for every pair of positions, so a text of n tokens gives "
            },
            {
              "text": "n² scores",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " for each head."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "n = 1,000: 1,000,000 scores"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "n = 2,000: 4,000,000 scores"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "n = 4,000: 16,000,000 scores"
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Doubling n makes attention roughly four times as expensive. The amount of arithmetic grows like n² times the vector width."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 37)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "quadratic-note",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The n² growth is a real limit on how long a context can be. A technique called FlashAttention gives exactly the same result while working through the matrix in small tiles held in fast memory, which cuts memory use and memory traffic."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The number of arithmetic operations stays quadratic even then. FlashAttention avoids storing the whole n × n matrix, it does not shrink n²."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 37)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Why are the attention scores divided by √d_k?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "correct": true,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Dot products grow like √d_k, and large scores push softmax into saturation. Dividing restores unit variance"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "That keeps the softmax from becoming an almost hard choice."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "To make the weights add up to 1"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The softmax does that, and it would anyway."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "To make the score matrix smaller than n × n"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The shape stays n × n."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "To remove the negative scores"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Dividing by a positive number does not change signs."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "teaches": ["big-scores", "divide"],
      "covers": ["score-scaling"],
      "variants": [
        {
          "prompt": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "How does the typical size of a dot product of two d_k-long vectors with unit-variance entries grow?"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "options": [
            {
              "correct": true,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "Like √d_k"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reason": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "The variance is d_k, so the typical size is its square root."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "correct": false,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "Like d_k"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reason": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "That is the variance. The typical size is its square root."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "correct": false,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "It does not depend on d_k"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reason": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "More terms in the sum make larger values."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "correct": false,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "Like d_k²"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reason": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "That grows faster than the dot product does."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
